Description: OpenBSD's bwfm on PIE
 bwfm drives the CYW43455 on the Raspberry Pi 4 and 500 through PIE's SDIO
 interface and PIE80211Family. The SDIO glue takes the card's functions and
 the bus claim from PIE's SDIO interface. Its bus work runs on PIEbwfm's
 bus thread, and the card's interrupt runs it with the claim held. Event
 and data frames go to a task in the family's gate, where net80211 runs.
 The transmit queue, its count and the control requests are kept under a
 lock that both sides take. A control request waits for its answer under
 that lock, so an answer that comes before the wait is not lost. An answer
 of the wrong length fails the request instead of freeing it while its
 sender still holds it. A request number stays within the 16 bits the
 protocol carries, skips one still waiting, and goes into its header as
 unsigned. All multicast frames are received, and the ioctls are the
 family's. An all-zero local-mac-address is not used. When the device tree
 gives an address, the NVRAM's own macaddr line goes, as the firmware takes
 the first, and the address follows the last entry, ended first if the file
 did not end it. The chip must end up with a valid address that is not the
 NVRAM's shared one, or the attach fails. A join that the firmware reports
 failed with E_SET_SSID, as when it finds no network, starts a new scan
 instead of staying in AUTH. Every scan is passive. The firmware's default
 is passive, a scan never asks for an SSID, and the join scans only the
 channel of the network chosen. A key that the firmware does not take
 leaves the port closed and starts a new scan. The chip's set-up is a
 function of its own, so that it can run again after a reset of the card,
 and preinit frees the media list and BSS node of the one before. Detach
 frees the events and frames still queued, and an attach that fails frees
 what it took. A function of its own sets the clocks for PIE's detach,
 which leaves the chip passive, so that its firmware does not stay
 associated and active with no driver loaded. Cleanup and a new firmware
 load free the CLM and other blobs still held. No newline ends the attach
 line, as PIE prints no autoconf line before it.
